{
  "headline": "Ghanaian minister commends progress of Agbogbloshie waste evacuation",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Mahama Ayariga, Ghana's Minister of Local Government, Chieftaincy and Religious Affairs, commended personnel from the 48 Engineer Regiment of the Ghana Armed Forces (GAF) for progress made during a visit to the Agbogbloshie waste evacuation site on October 16, 2026. The exercise, which was commissioned by President John Mahama on September 18, aims to clear tonnes of waste accumulated along the Odaw River and transport it to the Adepa Engineered Landfill site at Nsawam in the Eastern Region. During the visit, Ayariga encouraged the team to work to complete the exercise ahead of schedule and urged stakeholders to support government efforts to transform the area into an eco-tourism site. More than 100 machines, including those acquired for the District Road Improvement Programme (DRIP), were observed being used during the site visit."
    },
    {
      "text": "The evacuation team has cleared almost two mountains of waste at the Korle Bu side of the Odaw River, leaving one major pile remaining. On the Agbogbloshie side of the river, two more mountains of waste are scheduled for collection. While the 48 Engineer Regiment was commended, the waste evacuation is also being carried out by the 37 Engineering Regiment of the GAF. Once the three-month evacuation process is completed, the reclaimed site is intended for the construction of the Agbogbloshie Urban Renewal Project. Following the completion of the waste removal, the next phase of work involves dredging the polluted Odaw River to facilitate urban restoration and eco-tourism."
    }
  ]
}

{"headline": "Israeli settlers attack Palestinian olive farmers and Washington Post photographer in West Bank", "pages": 
[
  {
    "text": "Masked Israeli settlers wielding clubs, stones, and other weapons attacked Palestinians attempting to harvest olives in several West Bank towns on Friday. Palestinian authorities reported attacks in eight villages, resulting in injuries to at least 20 people. The violence occurred during the annual olive harvest and included the burning of cars and trees, according to various reports. In the town of Abu Falah, near Ramallah, at least 80 settlers—some carrying firearms or clubs—reportedly gathered to stop the harvest, using slingshots to hurl rocks at farmers in view of Israeli military vehicles. When some Palestinians picked up rocks to defend themselves, the Israeli military reportedly fired teargas in their direction. In the village of Kafr Malek, settlers allegedly raided a farm and killed approximately 20 sheep, while in the Beit Furik plain, settlers reportedly destroyed olive trees. In the Masafer Yatta area, the Palestine Red Crescent Society reported that six people, including four women and a child, were injured during an attack in Ma’in village on Saturday."
  },
  {
    "text": "Heidi Levine, a veteran photographer for The Washington Post, was among those injured during the attacks. Levine, who was wearing a flak jacket and helmet clearly marked as press, reported being attacked by more than 50 masked settlers near the villages of Abu Falah and Turmus Ayya. She stated she was pushed to the ground and kicked for more than 10 minutes before Palestinian colleagues intervened. Settlers also reportedly stole her passport, phone, wallet, and equipment. After receiving initial care in Ramallah, Levine was transferred to Beilinson Hospital in Petah Tikva, where doctors found she had suffered four broken vertebrae requiring surgery. The Washington Post expressed outrage at the attack and called for a thorough investigation. The Israeli Foreign Press Association stated that such violent attacks against journalists and civilians have been a recurring issue that has gone unaddressed by authorities. The office of Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu stated that the circumstances of the incident were unclear and under investigation, offering a version of events in which approximately 40 Israeli youths were confronted by some 200 Arabs and far-left activists, with both sides throwing objects."
  }
]
}

{
  "headline": "President Mahama commends Foreign Ministry for Ghana's UNHRC re-election",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "President John Dramani Mahama has commended Ghana's Minister of Foreign Affairs, Samuel Okudzeto Ablakwa, and the Ministry of Foreign Affairs for leading a successful campaign to secure re-election to the United Nations Human Rights Council (UNHRC). Speaking at the commissioning of new ambassadors at the Jubilee House in Accra on October 16, 2026, the President stated that the re-election for the 2027–2029 term places a renewed responsibility on the country to contribute with purpose and credibility. He expressed expectation that Ghana's Mission in Geneva will translate this confidence into constructive service on the council. The UNHRC is a 47-member body based in Geneva responsible for promoting and protecting human rights, addressing violations, and conducting the Universal Periodic Review. Ghana is currently serving a term that ends December 31, 2026, following its election in 2023."
    },
    {
      "text": "Ghana was re-elected for a second consecutive term during elections held by the UN General Assembly in New York on October 13, 2026, and October 14, 2026. The country secured 173 votes out of 181 valid ballots, placing it joint second globally alongside Kuwait and Nepal. Within the African group, Zambia topped the vote count with 177 votes, while Nigeria secured 172 votes. Ghana's term on the council is scheduled to run from January 1, 2027, to December 31, 2029. The campaign was led by the Foreign Ministry, utilizing Ghanaian missions worldwide to lobby for support."
    }
  ]
}

