{
  "headline": "HRW alleges Syrian authorities are restricting women's admission to defense and security university programs",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Human Rights Watch (HRW) stated on October 16, 2026, that Syrian authorities are discriminating against women by restricting their admission to most programs at two newly established state universities specializing in defense and security. The rights group urged the government to lift these restrictions before applications close on October 19. According to HRW, the Ministry of Higher Education and Scientific Research announced on September 30 that admission to law, economics and management, and social sciences programs at the National University of Defense Sciences is reserved for men. At the Syrian University for Security Sciences, women are reportedly only permitted to apply to cybersecurity programs. Hiba Zayadin, a senior Syria advisor for HRW, said the restrictions prevent women from training as military officers or pursuing careers as administrators and lawyers within the army. Zayadin stated that authorities are rebuilding the security forces and army without women."
    },
    {
      "text": "At the National University of Defense Sciences, men-only admission rules apply to the College of Humanities and Administrative Sciences, as well as the land, naval, and air force military colleges. The university’s Higher Institute for Applied Sciences and Technology is the only section open to women, though it does not require graduates to serve in the military. At the Syrian University for Security Sciences, the Technical Institute of Security Sciences and the College of Security Sciences also admit men only. HRW called on the Ministry of Higher Education to repeal gender-based admissions restrictions and open the programs to women on equal terms. The organization indicated that these policies contradict commitments made by transitional authorities to uphold women's rights and equality, and may undermine women's participation in Syria's political transition."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "TIB alleges state reforms in Bangladesh are being hindered by power politics and bureaucracy",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Transparency International Bangladesh (TIB) stated in a statement on October 17, 2026, that the potential for establishing an accountable government system and reforming the state following the July mass uprising is being hindered by bureaucratic influence and power politics. TIB Executive Director Iftekharuzzaman noted that while there was a commitment in the 31-point demand and election manifesto to implement state reforms and the July National Charter, uncertainty has arisen because members of parliament from the winning party did not take the oath as members of the proposed Constitutional Reform Commission. Iftekharuzzaman stated that the process of institutional reform, lawmaking, and constitutional amendment is now dependent on the inclinations of the ruling coalition. He further noted that of the 133 ordinances issued by the interim government, only those strengthening the executive branch have been passed into law, while ordinances intended to establish accountability have been suspended or repealed. TIB also reported that the opposition coalition is focused on partisan political interests rather than supporting state reform for the public interest."
    },
    {
      "text": "The TIB statement raised several specific concerns regarding judicial and institutional independence. The organization questioned the government's role in ensuring judicial independence, noting that the repeal of the Supreme Court Judges Appointment Ordinance and the Supreme Court Secretariat (Amendment) Ordinance represents a stance opposed to state reform. Regarding the Anti-Corruption Commission (ACC), TIB stated that failing to convert the ACC Amendment Ordinance into law and instead reverting to the 2004 law has obstructed efforts to overcome the commission's trust deficit. TIB also expressed concern over the National Human Rights Commission, stating that repealing its related ordinance to enact a new law conflicts with international standards and public aspirations for an independent commission. Additionally, the organization noted that the repeal of the Disappearances Prevention and Remedy Ordinance may protect agencies responsible for human rights violations. TIB criticized the decision to provide the Rapid Action Battalion (RAB) with a new identity to make it more powerful instead of dissolving it, and noted that no initiatives have been taken to form an independent police commission despite political consensus. Finally, Iftekharuzzaman observed that despite a consensus on elected representatives publishing asset details within three months of assuming office, no such practices have been reflected since members of parliament were sworn in."
    }
  ]
}

